The '''2013 corruption scandal in Turkey''' also known as the '''17–25 December Corruption and Bribery Operation''' was a series of criminal investigation launched in December 2013 involving businessmen, public officials and relatives of several cabinet ministers in the government of Prime Minister [[Recep Tayyip Erdoğan]]. The investigations included allegations of [[bribery]], [[corruption]], [[fraud]], [[money laundering]], [[gold]] [[smuggling]] and violations of [[International sanctions against Iran|sanctions against Iran]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Press |first=Associated |date=2014-02-25 |title=Turkish opposition calls for Erdogan to be investigated for corruption |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2014/feb/25/recep-tayyip-erdogan-investigated-corruption-turkey |access-date=2026-09-27 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> 

the first investigation became public on 17 December 2013, when Turkish police detained dozens of people, including the sons of three cabinet members, the general manager of the state owned [[Halkbank]], and [[Iran]]ian businessman [[Reza Zarrab]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=2013-12-21 |title=Turkey corruption inquiry: Ministers' sons charged |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-25475859 |access-date=2026-09-27 |work=BBC News |language=en-GB}}</ref> A second investigation was initiated on 25 December, but police did not carry out prosecutor's instructions to detain additional suspects. the investigation triggered a political crisis and were followed by the resignation of three cabinet members, a cabinet reshuffle and extensive reassignment and dismissal of police officers, prosecutors and judges involved in or in association with the investigation.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2014-01-07 |title=Hundreds of Turkish police officers dismissed |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-25634542 |access-date=2026-09-27 |work=BBC News |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Arango |first=Tim |date=2013-12-25 |title=Corruption Scandal Is Edging Near Turkish Premier |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/26/world/europe/turkish-cabinet-members-resign.html |access-date=2026-09-27 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> 

Erdoğan's government rejected the allegations and characterized the investigation as an attempted political operation by "parallel structure" associated with the Gülen movement. Critics of the government argued that the subsequent removal of investigators and changes to the judiciary judiciary interfered with the corruption investigations. The European Commission reported in 2014 that the government's response raised serious concern about judicial independence and the [[separation of powers]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Recknagel |first=Charles |date=2013-12-20 |title=Turkish Corruption Investigation Throws Government Into Crisis |url=https://www.rferl.org/a/turkey-corruption-scandal-crisis/25206516.html |access-date=2026-09-27 |work=Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty |language=en}}</ref>   

The principal December 2013 corruption investigation was discontinued in 2014 with prosecutors deciding not to pursue charges against 53 suspects. In January 2015, the Turkish parliament voted against sending four former ministers implicated in the allegations to the Constitutional Court for trial.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2013-12-20 |title=Regarding the Latest Corruption Scandal December, 2013 {{!}} Transparency International - Turkey |url=https://seffaflik.org/en/regarding-the-latest-corruption-scandal-december-2013/ |access-date=2026-09-27 |language=en-US}}</ref>

Subsequent proceedings in the United States involving Reza Zarrab and former Halkbank executive Mehmet Hakan Atilla addressed related allegation concerning the circumvention of [[United States sanctions against Iran|U.S. sanctions against Iran]]. Atilla was convicted in 2018.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Turkish Banker Convicted of Conspiring to Evade U.S. Sanctions Against Iran and Other Offenses |url=https://www.justice.gov/archives/opa/pr/turkish-banker-covicted-conspiring-evade-us-sanctions-against-iran-and-other-offenses |access-date=2026-09-27 |website=www.justice.gov|date=2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Cohen |first=Luc |last2=Cohen |first2=Luc |date=2026-07-14 |title=Key witness in US probe of Turkey's Halkbank avoids prison time |url=https://www.reuters.com/legal/government/key-witness-us-probe-turkeys-halkbank-avoids-prison-time-2026-07-14/ |access-date=2026-09-27 |work=Reuters |language=en}}</ref> 

==Police investigation and allegations==
On 17 December 2013, the Financial Crime Unit of the Istanbul Security Directorate detained 47 people, including officials from TOKİ (Housing Development Administration of Turkey), the [[Ministry of Environment and Urban Planning (Turkey)|Ministry of Environment and Urban Planning]], and the [[Fatih|District Municipality of Fatih]].<ref>{{cite news |url=http://sozcu.com.tr/2013/genel/buyuk-rusvet-ve-yolsuzluk-operasyonunda-gozaltina-alinanlarin-isimleri-425968/ |title=Büyük rüşvet operasyonunda gözaltına alınanların işlemleri sürüyor |language=tr |trans-title=Great bribery operation continues operations in detainees |newspaper=[[Sözcü]] |date=18 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014}}</ref> [https://www.hurriyetdailynews.com/photos-of-alleged-bribery-money-seized-in-house-of-turkish-ministers-son-revealed-59767 Barış Güler], [https://www.hurriyetdailynews.com/two-ministers-sons-arrested-in-turkeys-corruption-probe-59936 Kaan Çağlayan] and Oğuz Bayraktar, who are sons of the Turkish ministers, [[Muammer Güler]] ([[Ministry of the Interior (Turkey)|Minister of the Interior]]), [[Zafer Çağlayan]] (Minister of Economy), and [[Erdoğan Bayraktar]] ([[Ministry of Environment and City Planning (Turkey)|Minister of Environment and Urban Planning]]) were implicated, as well as Mustafa Demir, the mayor of the district municipality of Fatih; the real estate businessman [[Ali Ağaoğlu]]; Süleyman Aslan, the general manager of [[Halkbank]] and the [[Iran]]ian businessman [[Reza Zarrab]]<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.hurriyet.com.tr/ekonomi/25378685.asp |title=İstanbul'da yolsuzluk ve rüşvet operasyonu |language=tr |trans-title=Corruption and bribery operation in Istanbul |newspaper=[[Hürriyet Daily News]] |date=17 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014}}</ref> who was considered as the key suspect in the investigation and was reportedly involved in a money laundering scheme particularly regarding his transfer of gold and money to Iran via Turkey's government-controlled Halkbank as part of a strategy to bypass United States-led sanctions on Iran.<ref name="Turkey's Mother of All Corruption Scandals">[https://foreignpolicy.com/2015/01/06/why-turkeys-mother-of-all-corruption-scandals-refuses-to-go-away/ Why Turkey’s Mother of All Corruption Scandals Refuses to Go Away] - ForeignPolicy.com</ref> Demir was detained on accusations of approving the construction license for a hotel near the route of the recently inaugurated [[Marmaray]] railway, despite warnings from Japanese engineers that the construction could put the tunnel at risk of collapsing. Demir was investigated for ordering the ministry report to be shelved in exchange for bribes.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.hurriyetdailynews.com/turkish-pm-erdogan-says-those-who-target-him-through-graft-probe-will-not-succeed.aspx?pageID=238&nID=60154&NewsCatID=338|title=Turkish PM Erdoğan says those who target him through graft probe will not succeed - POLITICS}}</ref>

Moreover, [[Egemen Bağış]], the Minister of European Union Affairs, is cited in newspaper articles as a potential suspect of bribery related to [[Reza Zarrab]] who has business affiliations with [[Babak Zanjani]].<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.radikal.com.tr/turkiye/uc_bakana_rusvet_suclamasi-1166735 | title=Üç bakana rüşvet suçlaması | language=tr | trans-title=Three facing charges of bribery | date=18 December 2013 | newspaper=[[Radikal]] | access-date=18 December 2013 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131219021159/http://www.radikal.com.tr/turkiye/uc_bakana_rusvet_suclamasi-1166735 | archive-date=19 December 2013 | url-status=dead | df=dmy-all }}</ref><ref>{{cite news | url=http://t24.com.tr/haber/yolsuzluk-ve-rusvet-sorusturmasinda-egemen-bagis-goruntuleri-de-cikti/246398 | title=Yolsuzluk ve rüşvet soruşturmasında Egemen Bağış görüntüleri de çıktı! | language=tr | trans-title=Corruption and bribery investigation Egemen Bagis also output images! | date=19 December 2013 | publisher=T24 | access-date=19 December 2013 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131220012430/http://t24.com.tr/haber/yolsuzluk-ve-rusvet-sorusturmasinda-egemen-bagis-goruntuleri-de-cikti/246398 | archive-date=20 December 2013 | url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |author=Thomas Erdbrink | url=https://www.nytimes.com/2013/10/05/world/middleeast/to-this-tycoon-iran-sanctions-are-like-gold.html?_r=0| title=To This Tycoon, Iran Sanctions Were Like Gold| date=5 October 2013 | newspaper=[[The New York Times]] | access-date=5 October 2013}}</ref> As part of corruption claims along with 3 other ministers, Bağış was the only one out of the four charged ministers who had not resigned on 25 December 2013. Prime Minister Erdoğan announced a major cabinet reshuffle, removing Bağış from his post as the Minister for European Union through his publicly unannounced official resignation.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.resmigazete.gov.tr/main.aspx?home=http://www.resmigazete.gov.tr/eskiler/2013/12/20131227.htm&main=http://www.resmigazete.gov.tr/eskiler/2013/12/20131227.htm |title=BAKANLARIN İSTİFASI VE ATANMASINA DAİR İŞLEM |date=25 December 2013 |agency=T.C. Resmi Gazete |access-date=26 December 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131222025026/http://www.resmigazete.gov.tr/main.aspx?home=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.resmigazete.gov.tr%2Feskiler%2F2013%2F12%2F20131227.htm&main=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.resmigazete.gov.tr%2Feskiler%2F2013%2F12%2F20131227.htm |archive-date=22 December 2013 |df=dmy }}</ref>

The police confiscated some US$17.5 million as money used in bribery during the investigation; US$4.5 million was found at Süleyman Aslan's residence and US$750,000 at Barış Güler's.<ref>{{cite news |date=20 December 2013 |url=http://www.aktifhaber.com/operasyonda-ele-gecirilen-para-miktari-905081h.htm |title=Operasyonda Ele Geçirilen Para Miktari |language=tr |trans-title=The amount of money seized in operation |publisher=Aktif Haber |access-date=7 November 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150606215900/http://www.aktifhaber.com/operasyonda-ele-gecirilen-para-miktari-905081h.htm |archive-date=6 June 2015 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref> Prosecutors accused 14 people including Barış Güler, Kaan Çağlayan, Süleyman Aslan and Reza Zarrab of bribery, corruption, fraud, [[money laundering]] and [[smuggling]] gold. On 21 December, the court ordered the arrest of these 14 people.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.hurriyet.com.tr/gundem/25412674.asp |date=21 December 2013 |title=2 Bakan oğlu ile Reza Zarrab tutuklandı |language=tr |trans-title=Reza was arrested with two Ministers son Zarraba |newspaper=[[Hürriyet Daily News]] |access-date=7 November 2014 }}</ref>

In total, 91 people were detained in the investigation; 26 of them were arrested by the court.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.cumhuriyet.com.tr/haber/21119/Operasyonda_toplam_tutuklu_sayisi_ve_isimler.html |title=Operasyonda toplam tutuklu sayısı ve isimler |language=tr |trans-title=The total number of prisoners and the operation names |date=21 December 2013 |newspaper=[[Cumhuriyet]] |access-date=7 November 2014}}</ref>

===Second wave===
Several newspapers reported that a new investigation was expected on 26 December 2013, possibly involving then-Prime Minister (now [[President of Turkey]]) Erdoğan's sons, [[Bilal Erdoğan]] and [[Ahmet Burak Erdoğan|Burak]],<ref>{{cite news |url=http://haber.sol.org.tr/devlet-ve-siyaset/erdogan-oglunu-koruyor-sorusturma-savcidan-alindi-haberi-84919 |title=Erdoğan oğlunu koruyor: Soruşturma savcıdan alındı! |language=tr |trans-title=Erdoğan remains sons were taken from prosecutors investigating! |date=26 December 2013 |newspaper=[[SoL (newspaper)|SoL]] |access-date=7 November 2014}}</ref> as well as certain [[Al-Qaeda]] affiliates from [[Saudi Arabia]] such as Sheikh [[Yassin Kadi|Yaseen Al Qadi]] and Osama Khoutub.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.todayszaman.com/news-335038-report-al-qaeda-suspects-flee-after-turkish-govt-blocks-raid.html |title=Report: Al Qaeda suspects flee after Turkish gov't blocks raid |newspaper=[[Today's Zaman]] |date=26 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141108013004/http://www.todayszaman.com/news-335038-report-al-qaeda-suspects-flee-after-turkish-govt-blocks-raid.html |archive-date=8 November 2014 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref> The police officers in Istanbul Security Directorate, newly appointed by the government just a few days previously, refused to carry out their orders, and the deputy director of Public Prosecutions did not approve this new operation. The man behind this second investigation, Prosecutor [[Muammer Akkaş]], was dismissed on the same day.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://haber.gazetevatan.com/2-dalga-emniyete-takildi/595535/1/gundem |title=Savcı operasyondan alındı |language=tr |trans-title='Received from the Attorney-operation' |newspaper=[[Vatan (2002 newspaper)|Vatan]] |date=26 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131229191545/http://haber.gazetevatan.com/2-dalga-emniyete-takildi/595535/1/gundem |archive-date=29 December 2013 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Akkaş said he was prevented from performing his duty.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.todayszaman.com/news-335036-prosecutor-removed-from-new-graft-probe-amid-concerns-of-cover-up.html |title=Prosecutor removed from new graft probe amid concerns of cover-up |newspaper=[[Today's Zaman]] |date=26 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141108012858/http://www.todayszaman.com/news-335036-prosecutor-removed-from-new-graft-probe-amid-concerns-of-cover-up.html |archive-date=8 November 2014 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref>

A second wave of arrests was planned, and a list was [[News leak|leaked]] to the press.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://haber.sol.org.tr/devlet-ve-siyaset/iste-ikinci-dalgadaki-gozalti-listesi-haberi-84969 |title=İşte ikinci dalgadaki gözaltı listesi! |language=tr |trans-title=Here is the list of detainees in the second wave! |newspaper=[[SoL (newspaper)|SoL]] |date=27 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014 }}</ref>

At midnight on 7 January, a government decree was published removing 350 police officers from their positions, including the chiefs of the units dealing with financial crimes, smuggling and organised crime.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-25637710 |title=Turkish corruption probe row deepens |publisher=BBC News |date=7 January 2014 |access-date=7 November 2014}}</ref> [[Fethullah Gülen]] described the decree as a purge of civil servants, while then-[[Recep Tayyip Erdoğan|Prime Minister Erdoğan]] (now [[President of Turkey]]) described the corruption investigation as a "judicial coup" by those jealous of his success, backed by foreigners, namely a "dirty plot" of the [[Gülen movement]].<ref>{{cite news|agency=Reuters |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2014/jan/07/turkish-police-removed-posts-corruption |title=Hundreds of Turkish police removed from posts |newspaper=[[The Guardian]] |date=7 January 2014 |url-status=bot: unknown |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140110144248/http://www.theguardian.com/world/2014/jan/07/turkish-police-removed-posts-corruption |archive-date=10 January 2014 }}</ref>

==Government reaction==
Since the beginning of the investigation, the [[Justice and Development Party (Turkey)|Justice and Development Party (AKP)]] government has started a purge in the police force; sacking dozens of police chiefs, most notably [[Hüseyin Çapkın]], the Chief of Police in Istanbul.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.radikal.com.tr/turkiye/istanbul_emniyetinde_25_mudurun_yeri_degisti-1167417|title=İstanbul Emniyeti'nde 25 müdürün yeri değişti|language=tr |trans-title=Positions of the 25 police managers has changed in ıstanbul|newspaper=[[Radikal]] |date=22 December 2013|access-date=7 November 2014}}</ref> The Ministry of the Interior and the [[Ministry of Justice (Turkey)|Ministry of Justice]] also changed their regulations, forcing security forces to inform their seniors of their actions at all times.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.hurriyet.com.tr/gundem/25412900.asp |title=Adli Kolluk Yönetmeliği'nde değişiklik yapıldı |language=tr |trans-title=Changes were made to the Judicial Police Regulation |newspaper=[[Hürriyet Daily News]] |date=21 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014 }}</ref> This received criticism from the [[Turkish Bars Association|Union of Turkish Bar Associations]], who are taking the case to the [[Turkish Council of State]].<ref>{{cite news |author=Zeynep Gürcanli |url=http://www.hurriyet.com.tr/gundem/25426050.asp |title=Adli Kolluk Yönetmeliği'ne iptal davası |language=tr |trans-title=Regulation of Judicial Police action for annulment |newspaper=[[Hürriyet Daily News]] |date=23 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014}}</ref> The [[Turkish Council of State]] overturned the change of these regulations on 27 December, much to the disappointment of Prime Minister Erdoğan.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.hurriyet.com.tr/gundem/25453285.asp |title=Danıştay Adli Kolluk Yönetmeliği'ni durdurdu |language=tr |trans-title=Judicial Police stopped by the State Council Regulations |newspaper=[[Hürriyet Daily News]] |date=28 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014}}</ref>

Various opposition sources accused the government of trying to influence the judiciary system and covering up the corruption. Among them was the [[Nationalist Movement Party]] MP [[Oktay Vural]].<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.zaman.com.tr/gundem_mhpli-vural-hukumet-operasyonun-ustunu-ortup-delilleri-karartmaya-calisiyor_2185211.html |title=MHP'li Vural: Hükümet, operasyonun üstünü örtüp delilleri karartmaya çalışıyor |language=tr |trans-title=MHP Vural: The government is trying to evidence the top of the wrap operation blackout |agency=[[Xinhua News Agency]] |newspaper=[[Zaman (newspaper)|Zaman]] |date=18 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141108013733/http://www.zaman.com.tr/gundem_mhpli-vural-hukumet-operasyonun-ustunu-ortup-delilleri-karartmaya-calisiyor_2185211.html |archive-date=8 November 2014 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref> A heated controversy erupted after the release on [[YouTube]] of audio recordings in which Erdoğan was reportedly heard telling his son, Bilal, to urgently get rid of tens of millions of dollars. Erdoğan has claimed the recordings were faked but some experts disagreed with this claim.<ref name="Turkey's Mother of All Corruption Scandals"/><ref>{{Cite web |date=2014-11-19 |title=Experts say TÜBİTAK's report on recordings not scientific |url=http://www.todayszaman.com/national_experts-say-tubitaks-report-on-recordings-not-scientific_349867.html |access-date=2025-05-22 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141119112025/http://www.todayszaman.com/national_experts-say-tubitaks-report-on-recordings-not-scientific_349867.html |archive-date=19 November 2014 }}</ref>

In late December, ''[[Hurriyet]]'' and ''[[Yeni Safak]]'' papers published comments by Erdoğan stating he believes he is the ultimate target of a corruption and bribery probe of his allies. The Turkish Prime Minister told journalists that anyone attempting to enmesh him in the scandal would be "left empty handed." Erdoğan reshuffled his Cabinet on 25 Dec, replacing 10 ministers hours after three ministers, whose sons were detained in relation to the probe, resigned. One of those ministers called on Erdoğan to also step down. The ministers’ sons were questioned over the scandal focusing on alleged illicit money transfers to Iran and bribery for construction projects. Two of them were later arrested on bribery charges.<ref name="intimated.com">{{cite news|agency=Associated Press |title=Turkish PM Says He Is Target of Graft Probe |url=http://intimated.com/turkish-pm-says-he-is-target-of-graft-probe/ |publisher=Intimated |date=26 December 2013 |access-date=26 December 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131227111258/http://intimated.com/turkish-pm-says-he-is-target-of-graft-probe/ |archive-date=27 December 2013 }}</ref>

According to [[Hüseyin Çelik]], who is serving as government spokesman, the four ministers who are involved in the investigation offered their resignation to the Prime Minister [[Recep Tayyip Erdoğan|Erdoğan]] on 22 December.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.aksam.com.tr/siyaset/huseyin-celik-4-bakan-istifasini-sundu-sahidim/haber-270751 |title=Hüseyin Çelik: 4 Bakan istifasını sundu, şahidim |language=tr |trans-title=Hüseyin Celik: 4 offered the resignation of Ministers, I swear |newspaper=[[Akşam]] |date=21 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014}}</ref>

Erdoğan blamed the investigation on an international [[List of political conspiracies|conspiracy]] and vowed revenge on the [[Gülen movement|Islamic community]] of the preacher [[Fethullah Gülen]].<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.dokuzeylulgazetesi.com/haber/basbakan_ininize_girecegiz_didik_didik_edecegiz-4496.html |title=Başbakan: İninize Gireceğiz Didik Didik Edeceğiz |language=tr |trans-title=Prime Minister: We'll enter your Lair We Will Die |publisher=Dokuzeylulgazetesi.com |date=22 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014 |archive-date=8 November 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141108013716/http://www.dokuzeylulgazetesi.com/haber/basbakan_ininize_girecegiz_didik_didik_edecegiz-4496.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> There had been an antagonism between Erdoğan and Gülen for some time: [[Hakan Şükür]], who is often regarded as a disciple of Gülen, had resigned his post in Justice and Development Party on 16 December.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.trthaber.com/haber/gundem/hakan-sukur-istifa-etti-112463.html |title=Hakan Şükür istifa etti |language=tr |trans-title=Hakan Şükür resigns |date=16 December 2013 |publisher=TRT Haber |access-date=7 November 2014 |archive-date=22 December 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151222104922/http://www.trthaber.com/haber/gundem/hakan-sukur-istifa-etti-112463.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> Government officials accused Gülen and his followers of treason and started referring to them as "terrorists."<ref name="Turkey's Mother of All Corruption Scandals"/>

Erdoğan defended [[Halkbank]] chief executive [[Süleyman Aslan]], describing him as "honest person". Aslan was charged with taking bribes by the prosecutors while the police are said to reportedly have found US$4.5 million in cash stored in shoe boxes in Aslan's home.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.hurriyetdailynews.com/turkish-pm-erdogan-says-those-who-target-him-through-graft-probe-will-not-succeed.aspx?pageID=238&nID=60154&NewsCatID=338 |title=Turkish PM Erdoğan says those who target him through graft probe will not succeed |website=Hurriyetdailynews.com |date=2013-12-26 |access-date=2016-07-17}}</ref> After Aslan was arrested the bank's stock went down 32% for the year.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.businessinsider.com/turkey-gas-for-gold-deal-with-iran-2013-12 |title=Turkey Gas For Gold Deal With Iran |publisher=Business Insider |date=2013-12-27 |access-date=2015-12-29}}</ref> Erdoğan also threatened [[Francis J. Ricciardone, Jr.|Francis Ricciardone]], the [[United States Ambassador to Turkey|U.S. ambassador to Turkey]], with expulsion.<ref>{{cite news |author=Esra Kaya |url=http://www.hurriyet.com.tr/gundem/25418951.asp |title=Ülkemizde tutmayız |language=tr |trans-title=We do not keep our country |newspaper=[[Hürriyet Daily News]] |date=22 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014}}</ref>

Erdoğan left the country for an official visit to Pakistan on 23 December, while the scandal was dominating the headlines.<ref name="fireworks">{{cite news |author=Özgür Altuncu |title=Başbakan Erdoğan Pakistan'da havai fişeklerle karşılandı |language=tr |trans-title=Prime Minister Erdoğan was greeted in Pakistan with fireworks |newspaper=[[Hürriyet Daily News]] |url=http://www.hurriyet.com.tr/dunya/25423583.asp |date=23 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014}}</ref>

Muhammed Mısır, a bureaucrat in the [[Ministry of Health (Turkey)|Ministry of Health]], resigned after bribery allegations on 24 December.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.bugun.com.tr/son-dakika/saglik-bakanliginda-istifa-haberi/908999 |title=Sağlık Bakanlığı'nda Muhammed Mısır istifa etti |language=tr |trans-title=Minister of Health Muhammed Mısır has resigned |publisher=Bugun.com.tr |date=24 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141108013735/http://www.bugun.com.tr/son-dakika/saglik-bakanliginda-istifa-haberi/908999 |archive-date=8 November 2014 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref>

[[Muammer Güler]] (Minister of the Interior) and [[Zafer Çağlayan]] (Minister of Economy), both of whose sons were arrested in the anti-corruption operation, resigned together on the morning of 25 December.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.hurriyet.com.tr/gundem/25438044.asp |title=Bakan Çağlayan ile Güler istifa etti |language=tr |trans-title=Ministers Çağlayan and Güler resigned |newspaper=[[Hürriyet Daily News]] |date=25 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014}}</ref> That same afternoon, [[Erdoğan Bayraktar]] (Minister of Environment and Urban Planning) resigned both as minister and as a member of parliament. Bayraktar said that he did not take kindly to having been forced into resignation, and that Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdoğan should resign as well, claiming that he had done everything with the Prime Minister's approval.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.hurriyet.com.tr/gundem/25440241.asp |title=Erdoğan Bayraktar istifa etti |language=tr |trans-title=Erdoğan Bayraktar has resigned |newspaper=[[Hürriyet Daily News]] |date=25 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |author=Tim Arango |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/26/world/europe/turkish-cabinet-members-resign.html?pagewanted=1&_r=0&hp |title=Graft Scandal Is Approaching Turkey Premier |newspaper=The New York Times |page=A1 |date=26 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014}}</ref>

[[İdris Naim Şahin]], former Minister of the Interior, has resigned from Justice and Development Party on 25 December. He said the government's crackdown on the police organization and the judiciary system could not be explained with reason or the notions of law and justice.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.hurriyet.com.tr/gundem/25442203.asp |title=İdris Naim Şahin istifa etti |language=tr |trans-title=İdris Naim Şahin resigns |newspaper=[[Hürriyet Daily News]] |date=25 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014}}</ref>

450 policemen in the Financial Crimes department were forbidden to enter the Istanbul Security Department HQ.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://emedya.cumhuriyet.com.tr/haber/22841/450_polisin_Vatan_Emniyet_e_girmesi_yasaklandi.html# |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131227092703/http://emedya.cumhuriyet.com.tr/haber/22841/450_polisin_Vatan_Emniyet_e_girmesi_yasaklandi.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=2013-12-27 |title=450 polisin Vatan Emniyet'e girmesi yasaklandı |publisher=Emedya.cumhuriyet.com.tr }}</ref>

[[Erdal Kalkan]], a member of parliament in the constituency of [[İzmir]] from the ruling AKP, resigned from his party on 26 December because of the ongoing scandal.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.bbc.co.uk/turkce/haberler/2013/12/131226_kalkan_istifa.shtml |title=AKP'de iki milletvekili istifa etti |language=tr |trans-title=Two AKP deputies resign |publisher=BBC News |date=27 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014}}</ref> Haluk Özdalga, Member of Parliament in the constituency of Ankara, resigned from AKP for the same reason.<ref>{{cite news |date=27 December 2013 |url=http://sozcu.com.tr/2013/gundem/haluk-ozdalga-istifa-etti-431505/ |title=AKP Ankara Milletvekili Haluk Özdalga da istifa etti |language=tr |trans-title=AKP Ankara deputy Haluk Özdalga resigned |newspaper=[[Sözcü]] |access-date=7 November 2014}}</ref> [[Ertuğrul Günay]], another MP in the constituency of İzmir and former Minister of Culture, was the third one to resign from AKP.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.hurriyet.com.tr/gundem/25452913.asp |title=Ertuğrul Günay AKP'den istifa etti |language=tr |trans-title=Ertugrul Gunay resigned from the AKP |newspaper=[[Hürriyet Daily News]] |date=27 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014}}</ref> These three ex-members of AKP were each separately had been on investigation by AKP's internal discipline committee with the accusation of opposing AKP's own regulations. They all resigned before the committee reached a verdict.

On 31 December, [[Burdur Province|Burdur]] deputy [[Hasan Hami Yıldırım]] resigned from the AKP, reducing the number of AKP parliamentarians to 320. Yıldırım accused the government of obstructing the investigation into the scandal.<ref>{{cite news |author=Daniel Dombey |title=Turkey economy chief blames corruption probe for market fall |newspaper=[[Financial Times]] |url=https://www.ft.com/content/fb7534e2-722a-11e3-bff7-00144feabdc0 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140102131727/http://www.ft.com/cms/s/0/fb7534e2-722a-11e3-bff7-00144feabdc0.html |date=31 December 2013 |archive-date=2 January 2014}}</ref> He also criticized the campaign of “insults” and “defamation” against Islamic scholar [[Fethullah Gülen]] and his [[Hizmet movement]], which he considered an attempt to "eradicate a civil society movement.”<ref>{{cite news |title=Another AKP deputy, Hami Yıldırım, resigns in protest |agency=[[Cihan News Agency]] |url=https://www.cihan.com.tr/en/another-ak-party-deputy-hami-yildirim-resigns-in-protest-1243110.htm |date=31 December 2013 |access-date=20 May 2016 }}{{Dead link|date=September 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

Erdoğan's government dismissed or reassigned thousands of police officers and hundreds of judges and prosecutors - including those leading the investigation - and passed a law increasing government control of the judiciary.<ref>{{cite web|last=Tattersall |first=Nick |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-turkey-corruption-idUSBREA410NE20140502 |title=Turkey dismisses corruption case that has dogged PM Erdoğan |publisher=Reuters |date=2014-05-02 |access-date=2015-12-29}}</ref>

===Cabinet reshuffle===
In a speech to the press on the evening of 25 December, Prime Minister [[Recep Tayyip Erdoğan]] announced the reshuffle of 10 members of his [[Cabinet Erdoğan III|cabinet]]<ref name="intimated.com"/> in light of the scandal, saying that the reshuffle was to replace the three ministers who had resigned earlier in the day and others who were planning mayoral runs in the [[2014 Turkish local elections|local elections in March 2014]]. This included the removal of Minister for European Union and Chief Negotiator [[Egemen Bağış]], another figure in the scandal, from office.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.resmigazete.gov.tr/main.aspx?home=http://www.resmigazete.gov.tr/eskiler/2013/12/20131227.htm&main=http://www.resmigazete.gov.tr/eskiler/2013/12/20131227.htm |title=BAKANLARIN İSTİFASI VE ATANMASINA DAİR İŞLEM |date=25 December 2013 |agency=T.C. Resmi Gazete |access-date=26 December 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131222025026/http://www.resmigazete.gov.tr/main.aspx?home=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.resmigazete.gov.tr%2Feskiler%2F2013%2F12%2F20131227.htm&main=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.resmigazete.gov.tr%2Feskiler%2F2013%2F12%2F20131227.htm |archive-date=22 December 2013 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news | author1 = Orhan Coskun | author2 = Ece Toksabay | date = 25 December 2013 | title = Hit by scandal and resignations, Turk PM names new ministers | url = http://www.thestar.com.my/News/World/2013/12/26/Turkey-vows-no-coverup-despite-purge-of-graft-investigators.aspx | archive-url = https://archive.today/20131225224734/http://www.thestar.com.my/News/World/2013/12/26/Turkey-vows-no-coverup-despite-purge-of-graft-investigators.aspx | url-status=dead | archive-date = 25 December 2013 | newspaper = The Star Online | access-date = 25 December 2013}}</ref> The changes to the cabinet are:<ref>{{cite news | date = 26 December 2013 | title = Kabinede 10 bakan değişti | trans-title = 10 ministers in the Cabinet changed | url = http://www.zaman.com.tr/politika_kabinede-10-bakan-degisti_2189044.html | newspaper = Zaman Newspaper | access-date = 26 January 2013 | language = tr | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20131227061235/http://www.zaman.com.tr/politika_kabinede-10-bakan-degisti_2189044.html | archive-date = 27 December 2013 | url-status=dead | df = dmy-all }}</ref>

* [[Emrullah İşler]] – [[Deputy Prime Minister (Turkey)|Deputy Prime Minister]] – replacing [[Bekir Bozdağ]] (see below entry)
* [[Bekir Bozdağ]] – [[Ministry of Justice (Turkey)|Minister of Justice]] – replacing [[Sadullah Ergin]]
* [[Ayşenur İslam]] – [[Ministry of Family and Social Policy (Turkey)|Minister of Family and Social Policy]] – replacing [[Fatma Şahin]]
* [[Nihat Zeybekci]] – [[Ministry of Economy (Turkey)|Minister of Economic Affairs]] – replacing [[Zafer Çağlayan]] (who resigned)
* [[İdris Güllüce]] – [[Ministry of Environment and Urban Planning (Turkey)|Minister of Environment and Urban Planning]] – replacing [[Erdoğan Bayraktar]] (who resigned)
* [[Akif Çağatay Kılıç]] – [[Ministry of Youth and Sports (Turkey)|Minister of Youth and Sports]] – replacing [[Suat Kılıç]]
* [[Efkan Ala]] – [[Ministry of the Interior (Turkey)|Minister of the Interior]] – replacing [[Muammer Güler]] (who resigned)
* [[Lütfi Elvan]] – [[Ministry of Transport and Communication (Turkey)|Minister of Transport and Communication]] – replacing [[Binali Yıldırım]]
* [[Fikri Işık]] – [[Ministry of Science, Industry and Technology (Turkey)|Minister of Science, Industry and Technology]] – replacing [[Nihat Ergün]]
* [[Mevlüt Çavuşoğlu]] – [[Ministry of European Union Affairs|Minister for European Union Affairs]] – replacing [[Egemen Bağış]]

==Other controversies==
On 24 December, another video began to circulate that shows Ali Erdoğan, nephew and bodyguard of Prime Minister Erdoğan, instructing a police commissioner to abuse the detainees who had protested his uncle and hurling insults at him when he refuses.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.ulusalkanal.com.tr/gundem/erdoganin-yegeni-karakol-basti-h19848.html |title=Erdoğan'ın yeğeni karakol bastı |language=tr |trans-title=Erdoğan's nephew station raided |publisher=Ulusal Kanal |date=24 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141108013023/http://www.ulusalkanal.com.tr/gundem/erdoganin-yegeni-karakol-basti-h19848.html |archive-date=8 November 2014 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref>

On 23 December, 35-year-old Hakan Yüksekdağ, a police commissioner in the Smuggling and Battle Against Organized Crimes Department of the [[Ankara Province]] Security Directory, was found dead in his car. His relatives dispute the official claim that Yüksekdağ committed suicide.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://haber.sol.org.tr/devlet-ve-siyaset/emniyet-mudurunun-yakinlari-intihar-olamaz-buyuk-bir-operasyon-haberi-84724 |title=Emniyet Müdürü'nün yakınları: İntihar olamaz, büyük bir operasyon |language=tr |trans-title=Relatives Chief of Police: Suicide not a big operation |publisher=Sol |date=23 December 2014 |access-date=7 November 2014 }}</ref>

On 24 December, Abdi Altınok, an Assistant Chief of Police in the [[Isparta Province]] Security Directory, committed suicide.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.ntvmsnbc.com/id/25487798 |title=Emniyet Müdür Yardımcısı intihar etti |language=tr |trans-title=Deputy Chief Constable commits suicide |publisher=Ntvmsnbc |date=24 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014 |archive-date=8 November 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141108013736/http://www.ntvmsnbc.com/id/25487798 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Billionaire [[Babak Zanjani]], an alleged partner and accomplice of Reza Zarrab, was arrested in [[Iran]] on 30 December.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-middle-east-25551849 |title=Iranian billionaire Babak Zanjani 'arrested' |publisher=BBC News |date=30 December 2013 |access-date=7 November 2014}}</ref>

==United States prosecution==
{{Main|United States v. Atilla}}
Reza Zarrab, the businessman at the centre of the 2013 investigation, later became the defendant in a separate prosecution in the United States that continued for more than a decade after Turkey's own case was dismissed.

===Zarrab's arrest and guilty plea===
Zarrab was indicted in the United States in 2015.<ref name="occrp">{{cite web |title=Sanctions Evader Zarrab Avoids Prison After Aiding U.S. Prosecutors |url=https://www.occrp.org/en/news/sanctions-launderer-zarrabi-avoids-prison-after-aiding-us-prosecutors |website=Organized Crime and Corruption Reporting Project |access-date=2026-09-28}}</ref> He was arrested in Florida in March 2016 while on a family trip to Disney World and was later moved to New York to face a six-count indictment, which included charges of conspiracy to defraud the United States, conspiracy to commit bank fraud and conspiracy to commit international money laundering.<ref name="voa">{{cite news |title=Turkish-Iranian Gold Trader Pleads Guilty in Sanctions-busting Scheme |url=https://www.voanews.com/a/turkish-iranian-gold-trader-pleads-guilty/4140143.html |work=Voice of America |date=29 November 2017}}</ref> After initially pleading not guilty, he changed his plea and, on 26 October 2017, pleaded guilty to conspiracy, bank fraud and money laundering charges connected to a scheme to evade U.S. sanctions on Iran.<ref name="tm-zarrab">{{cite news |title=Key Halkbank witness Reza Zarrab avoids further prison time in US sanctions case: report |url=https://www.turkishminute.com/2026/07/14/key-halkbank-witness-reza-zarrab-avoids-further-prison-time-in-us-sanctions-case-report/ |work=Turkish Minute |date=14 July 2026}}</ref> Several co-defendants, including Halkbank's former general manager Süleyman Aslan and former Economy Minister Zafer Çağlayan, remained at large.<ref name="voa"/>

===Trial of Mehmet Hakan Atilla===
Zarrab testified for seven days for the prosecution against Mehmet Hakan Atilla, a deputy general manager at Halkbank, describing how Iranian oil revenue held in restricted accounts at the bank was moved through gold trades and fictitious food transactions.<ref name="tm-zarrab"/> He also testified that he had paid more than $50 million in bribes to a Turkish minister in 2012 and said he believed Erdoğan was aware of the scheme.<ref name="cbc">{{cite news |title=Turkish banker convicted in U.S. sanctions-busting trial |url=https://www.cbc.ca/1.4471788 |work=CBC News}}</ref> Turkish officials rejected the case as politically motivated; Erdoğan described it as an American conspiracy against Turkey.<ref name="cbc"/><ref>{{cite news |title=Gold trader's trial strains US-Turkey relations |url=https://gulfnews.com/world/mena/gold-traders-trial-strains-us-turkey-relations-1.2130711 |work=Gulf News}}</ref>

On 3 January 2018, after a four-week trial, a jury convicted Atilla on five of six counts, including conspiracy to defraud the United States and bank fraud.<ref name="doj">{{cite press release |title=Turkish Banker Convicted Of Conspiring To Evade U.S. Sanctions Against Iran And Other Offenses |url=https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdny/pr/turkish-banker-convicted-conspiring-evade-us-sanctions-against-iran-and-other-offenses |publisher=U.S. Attorney's Office, Southern District of New York |date=3 January 2018}}</ref> He was sentenced to 32 months in prison and returned to Turkey in 2019 after completing his sentence.<ref name="tm-zarrab"/>

===Halkbank prosecution and outcome===
In 2019, Halkbank itself was charged in a six-count indictment alleging fraud, money laundering and sanctions offenses.<ref name="occrp"/> U.S. prosecutors alleged that the bank had secretly transferred about $20 billion in restricted Iranian funds, converting oil revenue into gold and cash and using fake food shipments to disguise the transfers, with at least $1 billion passing through the U.S. financial system. The bank pleaded not guilty and denied wrongdoing.<ref name="tm-zarrab"/>

In 2026, the criminal case against Halkbank was dismissed under an agreement between the bank and the administration of President Donald Trump.<ref name="tm-zarrab"/><ref name="occrp"/> Less than a month later, Zarrab was sentenced by Judge Richard M. Berman. Prosecutors described his cooperation as substantial and reliable, and he received no additional prison term; his lawyers said he had spent 22 months in jail.<ref name="tm-zarrab"/><ref name="occrp"/> In a sentencing filing, Zarrab's lawyers also said that his family's assets in Turkey had been seized in retaliation for his cooperation.<ref name="occrp"/>

